    "q-221692": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221692",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the following false statement.\n\n$2∣5n-3$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer {{field-1}} such that {{field-2}} is {{field-3}} by $2.$",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,2∣5n-3.\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true.\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,2∣5n-3)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,2∤5n-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that $5n-3$ is $\\text{not divisible}$ by $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
