    "q-221708": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221708",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$2∣{n}^{3}+1$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n2{{field-4}}{n}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+1 & =({{field-6}})^{3}+1 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $2.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,2∣{n}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,2∣{n}^{3}+1)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,2∤{n}^{3}+1\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n2∤{n}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+1 & =(0)^{3}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $2.$ \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.\n\nPROOF\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that\n\n$$\n2\\,∤{n}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ $0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+1 & =(0)^{3}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $2.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ $0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
